{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001267","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates target ankylosing spondylitis, but they split into (i) UK Biobank pan-trait sparse snpnet scores with explicit PRS-only AUROC/R² (PGS001267/PGS001268), (ii) UKB portability paper scores reporting only partial correlation (PGS001876/PGS002089), and (iii) small East Asian Taiwanese scores reporting only full-model AUROC with unknown covariates (PGS003420–PGS003424).

PGS001267 is preferred because it is a direct endpoint match (phenotyping_reported: ankylosing spondylitis), has clean/basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs), and—critically—reports PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC 0.7265; PGS-only R² 0.0988) in a large European validation (n=67,425). The time-to-event sibling PGS001268 has slightly higher PRS-only metrics, but time-to-event phenotyping is a mild endpoint-fidelity negative for a generic disease-risk target when the alternative is an otherwise identical direct case/control-style endpoint; the performance difference is small enough that endpoint fidelity breaks the tie.

Heritability context (EUR h2_liability estimates ~0.14–0.40 depending on definition) does not contradict the reported PRS-only R² (~0.10), so the performance looks plausible rather than obviously covariate-driven or mis-scaled. Overall, PGS001267 offers the best combination of direct phenotype alignment and the most interpretable, PRS-only performance evidence among the fixed candidates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001135","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"For the target trait "arthritis" (broad/umbrella), the only candidate that directly matches the same umbrella endpoint is PGS001135 (trait_reported: Arthritis (nos); trait_efo: arthritis; phenotyping_reported: Arthritis (nos)). Most other candidates are for specific arthritis subtypes (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis, juvenile idiopathic arthritis, osteoarthritis, gout), which are not equivalent to generic arthritis.

PGS001135 reports PRS-only metrics (PGS AUROC no covariates = 0.539; PGS R2 no covariates = 0.00291) plus full-model AUROC 0.674 with basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). The incremental AUROC is small (0.00314), indicating limited standalone PRS contribution—consistent with local heritability records for broad/self-reported arthritis phenotypes being low (UKB self-reported arthritis (nos) h2 on observed scale ~0.0006–0.0045; ICD10 M13 other arthritis h2 ~0.002–0.007). This makes the choice direct but not high-performing.

Other arthritis-EFO candidates (PGS004477, PGS004547) are also labeled "arthritis" but provide only an OR without clear PRS-only discrimination metrics and have unknown covariates, making them less comparable/less informative than PGS001135 for selecting a best-performing generic arthritis score."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000327","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"PGS000327 is the clean direct match to the target trait: trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported are all “Autism spectrum disorder,” whereas PGS002790 has an endpoint-fidelity concern (phenotyping_reported is “Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn)”), making it less clearly an ASD diagnosis score despite sharing the ASD label fields.

On performance evidence, neither model reports PRS-only AUC/R², so we rely on the most comparable available signals. PGS000327 reports an OR per SD of 1.33 (moderate PRS signal) and an R²=0.0245 (likely a full-model/variance-explained metric but still supportive given basic covariates: genetic PCs, genotyping wave). PGS002790 reports only a partial R² of ~0.0055 with basic covariates (age, PCs1-3) and no effect size, which is weaker evidence of PRS contribution.

Study/context signals also favor PGS000327: it comes from a major ASD GWAS paper (Nat Genet) with explicit ASD-focused training cohorts and a larger stated training sample (n=28,592) and validation size (n=7,148) than PGS002790 (validation n=2,198; training N/A). Heritability records for ASD (EUR h2 roughly ~0.19–0.29 in GWAS Atlas) are consistent with PGS000327’s modest explained variance being plausible and not obviously inflated by covariates."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS002042","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Both candidates are direct matches to the target (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all “Corneal dystrophy”) and come from the same UK Biobank portability paper with the same training N (391,124), validation ancestry context, validation N (19,321), and only basic covariates (sex, age, birth date, deprivation index, PCs), so endpoint fidelity and covariate comparability do not differentiate them.

Performance evidence is limited to the selected European validation “partial correlation (partial-r)”, where PGS002042 is higher (0.0471; CI 0.033–0.0611) than PGS001835 (0.0384; CI 0.0243–0.0525). With no heritability records available locally for corneal dystrophy, we cannot further sanity-check these magnitudes against an h2 ceiling.

Given otherwise matched study context, the higher partial-r plus the more polygenic LDpred2 construction with far more variants (59,944 vs 38) is the stronger within-family choice."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS001331","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates align to Crohn disease at the EFO level, but PGS001330 is explicitly a time-to-event endpoint (a mild endpoint-fidelity warning for a generic Crohn’s disease target), whereas PGS001331 is a straightforward Crohn’s disease case/control-style label with matching phenotyping_reported. Between the two UKB snpnet siblings (PGS001330 vs PGS001331), PGS001331 has slightly better PRS-comparable performance (PGS-only AUROC 0.579 vs 0.576; PGS-only R² 0.00677 vs 0.00621) and a slightly higher incremental AUROC, with comparable basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs) and identical training/validation scale.

PGS004254 is also a direct-label Crohn’s disease score and uses a modern genome-wide method (LDpred2-auto; ~745k variants), but its reported discrimination is only a full-model AUROC (0.72) without PRS-only AUROC/R², making it hard to compare fairly against the snpnet models that do report PRS-only metrics; the full-model AUROC could be partly covariate-driven. Its OR per SD (2.18) is strong supportive evidence, but given the metric comparability advantage and clean endpoint match of PGS001331, PGS001331 is the most defensible “best” choice from the fixed list.

Heritability context: external EUR h2/liability estimates for Crohn’s disease are substantial (e.g., h2_liability ~0.18–0.23 in GWAS Atlas records), so the modest PRS-only R² reported for the UKB-derived snpnet scores is plausible and not suspiciously inflated; this supports trusting the PRS-only metrics for within-list ranking."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002304","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Target trait is follicular lymphoma. Endpoint fidelity: PGS002304 has trait_reported and phenotyping_reported both exactly “Follicular lymphoma”, matching the target cleanly. By contrast, PGS000643/PGS000644 are labeled “Nodular lymphoma” in trait_reported/phenotyping_reported (though mapped to follicular lymphoma in trait_efo), and PGS003455 has a clear phenotype mismatch (phenotyping_reported = chronic lymphocytic leukemia), making it unreliable for this target.

Performance evidence: none of the candidates report PRS-only AUC/R², so full-model AUROC must be treated cautiously. Still, PGS002304 shows materially higher reported AUROC (0.61, CI 0.59–0.63) than the PRSweb scores (0.532–0.538 with basic covariates), suggesting stronger discrimination in its evaluation setting. Its validation sample size is small (n=197), which lowers confidence, but it remains the best direct-match option given the combination of exact endpoint alignment and the strongest available discrimination signal.

Heritability context: local EUR h2_liability estimates for follicular lymphoma/ICD10 C82 are modest (~0.16–0.38 across records), consistent with limited achievable predictive power; the PRSweb full-model AUROCs near 0.53 look plausibly weak/near-noise, while 0.61 is more consistent with a detectable genetic signal (though still needing larger validation for stability)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004911","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All candidates are concept-aligned to hypertrophic cardiomyopathy by trait_reported/trait_efo, but endpoint fidelity differs: PGS000778 is evaluated on “clinical events in individuals with a pathogenic/likely pathogenic sarcomeric variant,” which is a restricted high-risk subgroup rather than general HCM status, making it less directly deployable for generic HCM risk. PGS000739, PGS004910, and PGS004911 all use a direct HCM phenotype.

On performance evidence, none report PRS-only AUROC/R², so full-model metrics must be interpreted cautiously (they can be covariate-driven). PGS000739’s AUROC=0.821 is from a model with heavy clinical covariates (multiple comorbidities/risk factors plus rare-variant carrier status), so it is not strong evidence of standalone PGS quality. In contrast, PGS004910/PGS004911 are evaluated with basic covariates (age, age^2, sex, PCs), making their full-model AUROC/R² more comparable and less “packaged.” Between those two, PGS004911 (MTAG) is mildly preferred per endpoint-label guidance (MTAG is a power-boosting enhancement, not a mismatch) and it shows stronger reported metrics in the same setting (AUROC 0.80 vs 0.73; R² 0.048 vs 0.031; slightly stronger OR/HR). Variant counts and method family are essentially identical (PRS-CS genome-wide).

Heritability records available locally are only for the broader label “Cardiomyopathy” (not a close match), so they don’t strongly constrain interpretation here; they mainly reinforce that we should not over-trust covariate-heavy AUROC. Overall, PGS004911 is the best-supported direct HCM score in this fixed list."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS004492","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Target trait is kidney failure. Among candidates, the most endpoint-aligned records are those with trait_efo=kidney failure: PGS000708 (Kidney failure; phenotyping is diabetic kidney failure), and the ICD-coded chronic renal failure models PGS004492 (LDpred2; N18 Chronic renal failure) and PGS004562 (RFDiseasemetaPRS; N18 Chronic renal failure). PGS004492/PGS004562 are the cleanest direct matches to generic kidney failure because they are explicitly chronic renal failure (a close synonym/instantiation of kidney failure) without restricting to a diabetic-only subtype (as in PGS000708).

Between the two N18 kidney-failure candidates, PGS004562 shows a stronger reported per-SD effect size (OR≈1.40) than PGS004492 (OR≈1.17), which is supportive of better PRS discrimination when AUC/R2 are not reported. However, PGS004562 is a metaPRS/ensemble-style model with unknown covariates and no discrimination metrics, which lowers interpretability and comparability; PGS004492 is a standard LDpred2 disease PRS (more straightforward standalone PGS) with the same training/validation ancestry context (EUR-only) and similarly missing AUC/R2. Given the goal of selecting a single best deployable PGS for kidney failure from this list, I prioritize direct endpoint fidelity plus methodological simplicity/clarity over a single stronger OR from a more composite model with unknown packaging.

Heritability records for kidney failure (UKB self-reported renal/kidney failure) suggest very low observed-scale h2, so very high full-model discrimination would likely be covariate-driven; since the N18 candidates do not rely on covariate-inflated full-model AUC reporting, they remain acceptable direct picks despite limited metric reporting. Overall, PGS004492 is the best-balanced direct-match candidate, but confidence is only moderate because none of the kidney-failure-labeled models report PRS-only AUC/R2 and covariates are unknown for the chosen record."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS004163","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Target trait is rheumatoid arthritis; prioritize candidates whose phenotyping_reported is a direct RA endpoint (or a clinically close RA subtype) and whose reported performance is interpretable as largely PRS-driven (ideally PRS-only metrics, otherwise clean covariates). Several candidates are non-matches (juvenile idiopathic arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis) and are deprioritized.

Among direct RA candidates, the 2023 Am J Hum Genet “five biobanks / ensemble learning” cluster (PGS003994, PGS004010, PGS004021, PGS004049, PGS004064, PGS004079, PGS004094, PGS004103, PGS004118, PGS004133, PGS004148, PGS004163) reports performance with covariates=0, making the AUROC/R² much more comparable as PRS-driven than scores reporting only full-model AUROC with covariates. Within this cluster, PGS004163 (UKBB-EUR.MultiPRS.CV) has the strongest reported discrimination and variance explained (AUROC ~0.747; R² ~0.138) in European validation (n~90,274), exceeding the other RA models in the same family (e.g., LDpred2.CV AUROC ~0.728; R² ~0.122). The reported R² is also plausible relative to external RA SNP-heritability benchmarks (EUR h2_liability ~0.10–0.14 in large GWAS Atlas records), supporting that the metric is not obviously inflated.

Other direct RA options are weaker on comparable evidence: UKB pan-trait snpnet RA scores (PGS001309/PGS001310) have much lower PRS-only AUROC (~0.60) and R² (~0.015); portability-paper RA scores (PGS001875/PGS002088) report only partial correlation (harder to compare); and some high full-model metrics (e.g., incident RA C-index 0.85 in PGS004873) are not directly comparable and come with atypical training context. Therefore PGS004163 is the best-supported direct RA PGS in this fixed list."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS002075","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Target trait is urolithiasis. Endpoint fidelity: PGS001864 and PGS002075 are direct matches (trait_efo=urolithiasis; phenotyping_reported=Urinary calculus). PGS001250 is close but uses a time-to-event endpoint (TTE calculus of kidney and ureter), which is a mild negative when direct case/control-style endpoints exist. PGS004493/PGS004563 are nephrolithiasis/N20 kidney+ureter calculus and report only per-SD ORs with unknown covariates, making them harder to compare.

Performance evidence: PGS001250 reports PRS-only AUROC=0.5668 and PRS-only R²=0.00592 (European validation), which is usable but modest. PGS001864 and PGS002075 do not report AUROC/R²; they report partial correlation (partial-r), where higher is better within the same study/phenotype/covariate setup. Between those two portability-study siblings (same paper, same training cohort UKB, same covariates, same validation ancestry), PGS002075 has higher partial-r (0.0385 vs 0.0323), indicating stronger PRS signal.

Method/structure: PGS002075 uses LDpred2 with ~731k variants, typically capturing more polygenic signal than penalized regression with ~5.6k variants (PGS001864) when trained comparably. Given direct endpoint match and the strongest available comparable metric among the direct-match pair, PGS002075 is the best-supported choice. Heritability records were unavailable for urolithiasis here, so confidence is moderated."}
